{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>For it is rebellion against YHWH your Elohim, who brought you out of the land of Egypt.</b> That is to say, he is a rebel against the kingdom of heaven. And even if he performed several signs and wonders and made the sun stand still in the middle of the firmament and said that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded him that we today worship a star or a constellation or one of the other supernal powers — he is liable to death by strangulation, for he has come to deny the prophecy of Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, which is publicized and clarified to all Israel, and likewise he has come to deny the utterance (Exodus 20) ״לא יהיה לך״, which all Israel heard with their own ears, and they know and are witnesses concerning this matter. Therefore the Torah says, ״he shall be put to death״; and everything he did was all by way of wisdom and sorcery." "<b>And behold,</b> this is in the matter of idolatry alone, where we do not believe him at all and pay no attention to any sign or wonder he may perform. But regarding other commandments, if he says to us that we should today transgress a particular commandment because the Holy One, blessed be He, so commanded him — since he was previously established among us as a prophet and walks in the ways of prophecy and saintliness, we must believe him, since he is not commanding us to transgress that commandment for all generations and to uproot it entirely, but rather issues a temporary ruling (hora'at sha'ah) to this effect. We are commanded to listen to him; this is what is written (Deuteronomy 18): ״אליו תשמעון״. And the reason we rely upon his sign, although it is possible that he did it by way of wisdom and sorcery and he is not a prophet, is that we follow his good presumption (chezkatoh); since he was previously established among us as a prophet, we must maintain him in his presumption — just as we rely upon two witnesses who are qualified in our eyes, although it is possible that they are false witnesses; rather, we maintain the witnesses in their presumption. So too here, with this prophet, we must maintain him in his presumption. And concerning this the Torah says (Deuteronomy 29): ״הנסתרות לה' אלהינו והנגלות לנו ולבנינו״. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: in every case, if a prophet says to you, transgress the words of Torah — listen to him, like Elijah at Mount Carmel — except for idolatry. And Elijah's matter was a temporary ruling: he offered a burnt offering outside the Temple in order to refute the prophets of Baal, and he did not intend to uproot the commandment; had he so intended, it would be forbidden to listen to him, for all the commandments are eternal and were given for all generations, as it says (ibid.): ״לנו ולבנינו עד עולם לעשות את כל דברי התורה הזאת״. And what the Sages, of blessed memory, mentioned concerning Elijah applies equally to all other prophets who were commanded by the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, to transgress the commandments temporarily — as Joshua did, who was commanded to conquer Jericho on the Sabbath, and did so temporarily, not that he was commanded to uproot the commandment of the Sabbath, heaven forbid. And so we find with Elisha, who commanded (II Kings 3): ״וכל מעין מים יסתמו וכל עץ טוב יפילו״, and the Torah says (Deuteronomy 20): ״לא תשחית את עצה״ — rather, for that time alone he so commanded, not to uproot the commandment; if he intended that, we do not listen to him. And it goes without saying regarding uprooting the commandment entirely — but even to add to it or to subtract from it, it is not fitting for us to believe this through the prophecy of any prophet. And this is the explanation of what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: if Elijah comes and says, ״one removes the halitzah shoe with a sandal (חולצין במנעל)״ — we listen to him; ״with a slipper (בסנדל)״ — we do not listen to him. And further in tractate Berakhot: if Joshua son of Nun had said it to me from his own mouth, I would not have obeyed him. And even if we heard a bat kol from heaven concerning the commandments, we do not listen to the bat kol to uproot a commandment, for the commandment came to us through prophecy, which is superior to a bat kol; therefore we would not heed the bat kol. And concerning this they said in Bava Metzi'a: R. Joshua stood on his feet and said: ״You wrote for us in Your Torah (Exodus 23): אחרי רבים להטות.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:15.590455011Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}